meta {
  name: Update guardian link
  type: http
  seq: 29
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/guardians/{{guardianId}}/students/{{studentId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "is_emergency_contact": true,
    "can_view_academic": true
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 403
}

docs {
  403 is what this answers today, and the assert says so deliberately.

  The route is gated by core.student_guardians.write, which is not registered in
  CAS yet, so the guard refuses the capability before any row is read and the
  body replies "not permitted: core.student_guardians.write". Registering that
  capability in CAS and granting it is the only thing standing between this and
  a 200 - the handler itself is finished. When that happens this assert must be
  changed to 200, and it will fail loudly in the meantime rather than quietly
  passing against a route nobody can call.

  Read the 403 carefully: it is about the capability, not about this particular
  guardian or this particular child. A caller who holds the capability but
  cannot reach the guardian or the student gets 404 instead - see request 13.

  Why the route matters. can_view_academic on this link gates real access, not
  a UI checkbox: with it false, this guardian's own token cannot reach the
  ward's enrolments, subject enrolments, student assignments or documents, even
  though CAS granted them core.students.read for their children. The body here
  re-affirms the values request 09 already set, so that the run stays
  reproducible if this ever starts succeeding.

  Two things the route will not do even once it is allowed: student_id comes
  from the path and sending it in the body is a 400, because moving a link to a
  different child is deleting one relationship and creating another; and
  editing a link needs core.guardians.write and core.students.read as well,
  since a caller who cannot see the child has no business editing who may
  collect them.
}
